`,t.jsxs(e.p,{children:["For much of the 2000s and early 2010s, many sites followed a familiar pattern: the server rendered HTML, the browser displayed it, and JavaScript improved interactivity where needed. ",t.jsx(e.strong,{children:"jQuery"})," made that layer approachable across inconsistent browsers. ",t.jsx(e.strong,{children:"WordPress"}),", ",t.jsx(e.strong,{children:"Drupal"}),", and similar CMS platforms made publishing fast for millions of sites. That stack still works for a large share of the web today."]}),`
`,t.jsxs(e.p,{children:["What changed was ",t.jsx(e.strong,{children:"user expectations"}),". Search-as-you-type, live dashboards, collaborative editing, and mobile apps raised the bar for interactivity. Teams building long-lived ",t.jsx(e.strong,{children:"applications"})," — not only articles and brochures — often adopted component-based JavaScript frameworks. ",t.jsx(e.strong,{children:"React"})," became one of the most widely used options for that job. ",t.jsx(e.strong,{children:"React Native"})," extended a similar component model to iOS and Android."]}),`
`,t.jsx(e.p,{children:"This article discusses those shifts in neutral terms. jQuery, WordPress, and React solve overlapping but not identical problems. Preferences here reflect the author's experience; your stack should match your product, team skills, and maintenance horizon."}),`
`,t.jsx(e.h2,{children:"What jQuery did well — and where larger apps strained"}),`
`,t.jsxs(e.p,{children:[t.jsx(e.a,{href:"https://jquery.com/",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",children:"jQuery"})," remains maintained and appropriate for many tasks. It unified DOM APIs, simplified events and AJAX, and helped a generation of developers ship interactivity quickly. For small enhancements on server-rendered pages, it is still a reasonable choice."]}),`
`,t.jsxs(e.p,{children:["Difficulties tended to appear as ",t.jsx(e.strong,{children:"application complexity"}),' grew. Imperative DOM updates — select an element, mutate it, bind another handler — can scatter state across the page. In large codebases, answering "what is on screen?" may require tracing selectors instead of reading a single state model. That pain point motivated declarative libraries, including React, Vue, and others — not a failure of jQuery itself, but a different scale of problem.']}),`
